    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d91-a1">

                <pb facs="0229" n="157" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg157"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 301.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Messrs. William Archdeacon &amp; Co. to Mr.
                        Frelinglmysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Indianapolis</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-02-05"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 5,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: We received an order early in December
                    from Altona, Germany, for 100 barrels of pigs’ tongues, and shipped same early
                    in January. We are now in receipt of a letter from the bank in Hamburg saying
                    “that American pork has been prohibited in Germany,” and our goods will not be
                    allowed to land there.</p>
                <p>The shippers and shipping agents from whom we have sought information deny that
                    such is the case, and our goods in the mean time lie there.</p>
                <p>If you can give us any information on the subject you will confer a favor on</p>
                <p>Yours respectfully,</p>
                <closer>WM. ARCHDEACON &amp; CO.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
